<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Parliament of Finland</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1907">1907</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Eduskunta</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Parliament of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>, or Eduskunta, is the country's single-chamber legislature of 200 members. Created by the reform of 1906, which replaced the Diet of four estates while Finland was a grand duchy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, it was elected by universal suffrage. Finland thus became the first European country to give women both the right to vote and to stand for election.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/parliament-of-finland.jpg" alt="Parliament of Finland" width="320" height="207" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jukuboi · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eduskunta_istumaj%C3%A4rjestys_2023.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Parliament of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> was preceded by the Diet of Finland, which had succeeded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riksdag-of-the-estates">Riksdag of the Estates</a> in 1809 and was tetracameral. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diet-of-porvoo">Diet of Porvoo</a> of 1809 met in several buildings in Porvoo, assembled only once and did not reconvene until 1863. When the unicameral Parliament was established by the Parliament Act in 1906, Finland was an autonomous grand duchy under the Russian tsar, who ruled as Grand Duke and not as an absolute monarch. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/universal-suffrage">Universal suffrage</a> and eligibility were introduced: women could vote and run for office as equals, as could landless people, with no minority excluded. The first election was held in 1907, and the first Parliament had 19 female representatives, a number without precedent, which grew to 21 by 1913.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first years were difficult. Between 1908 and 1916 the power of the Parliament was almost completely neutralised by Tsar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii">Nicholas II</a> and the so-called sabre senate, a bureaucratic government formed by Imperial Russian Army officers during the second period of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russification">Russification</a>. The Parliament was dissolved and new elections held almost every year. In the 1916 election the Social Democrats won a majority, the only time one party has held a parliamentary majority, and coalition governments have therefore been the rule. Until the Constitution Act of 1919 the body was known in Swedish as lantdagen, the old term for the Diet.</p><p>The Parliament has been dissolved before the end of its term fourteen times; the most recent was on 4 June 1975. It first met in a volunteer fire brigade house at Keskuskatu 7, and in 1911 moved to the Heimola House, designed by Onni Tarjanne. Both buildings were later demolished. The Parliament House now stands in central Helsinki.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Parliament has 200 members, 199 elected every four years from 13 multi-member districts by the proportional D'Hondt method, plus one member from Åland. It passes legislation, decides the state budget, approves international treaties and supervises the government, which it can bring down. It may override presidential vetoes, and changes to the constitution need approval by two successive parliaments with an election between them. Most members work in parliamentary groups that correspond to political parties.</p><p>Its 17 committees include the Grand Committee, which deals with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> matters, and the Constitutional Law Committee, which oversees constitutional questions because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> has no constitutional court. The Committee for the Future assesses long-term developments. Each year the Parliament elects a speaker and two deputy speakers on the first day of the session, and members enjoy parliamentary immunity.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Parliament of Finland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The parliamentary reform of 1906 created a single-chamber parliament, the <b>Eduskunta</b>, elected by universal suffrage, the first in Europe to give women both the vote and the right to stand.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-finland">Republic of Finland · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
